  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nToday Though Sunday...\n\nMild and mainly dry weather conditions will continue for the next 36 \nhours. Sfc high pressure will continue to push off to the east \nduring the day and then off the New England coast by tomorrow night. \nWhile a north to south oriented cold front stretching from MN to NM \nwill slowly work eastward towards the region during this period. \nUpper ridging aloft will remain over the region today helping to \nkeep our temperatures mild and well above normal. Temperatures at \n850mb will be around 10 degrees C allowing for us to see another \nafternoon with temperatures into the low 70s to upper 60s. \n\nBy Sunday, the upper ridging shifts eastward and the sfc cold front \nalong with an upper level trough along the US/Canada border push \neastward. The daytime hours continue to look mainly dry and remain \nmild with highs again in the upper 60s to near 70 as winds become \nmore south-southwest and may increase a little as the sfc front gets \ncloser. High clouds are expected to increase late in the day into \nthe early evening ahead of the approaching boundary. \n\nSunday Night into Monday Morning... \n\nSfc cold front will spread a line of broken showers into the area \nfrom the west Sunday evening with models continue to suggest the \nprecipitation breaks and diminishes as it moves east of I-65 and \ninto the Bluegrass Sunday night. Precipitation amounts look to be \nrather low ranging from around 0.10\" across all of southern IN and \nalong and west of I-65. It then rapidly drops down to just a couple \nhundreths of an inch east of I-65 into the Bluegrass. Current timing \nfor FROPA from west to east is between 00z to around 08z. Winds will \nshift from the south to the northeast along the sfc boundary with \npossible winds gusts of 15-20 mph and can't rule out some isolated \ngusts near 30 mph. Temperatures will fall into 40s and even upper \n30s by Monday morning. \n\nMonday and Tuesday... \n\nSfc high pressure dropping out of Saskatchewan Canada will follow \nbehind the departing cold front and will allow for much colder air \nto advect into the Ohio Valley for Monday and Tuesday. Highs will go \nfrom the low/mid 50s on Monday to struggling to get out of the 40s \non Tuesday. Lows will be in the low 30s to upper 20s. The colder air \nis expected to remain across the area through the week. \n\nWednesday into the Thanksgiving Holiday...  \n\nConfidence continues to grow that we will see increase precipitation \nchances for Wednesday into Thanksgiving as the deterministic models \nstart to come into a little more alignment on a couple of upper \nlevel disturbances expected mid to late week. While current guidance \nkeeps overall impacts low with precipitation mostly in the form of \nrain there is still low probs (less than 20 percent) that some snow \ncould mix in late Wednesday into Thanksgiving. At this point, the \nmain impacts look to be more along the east coast as the models want \nto develop a Nor'easter for Thanksgiving.",
